            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a patron, then the Latin for evidence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: iūrō, iūrāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "iūrō, iūrāre — \"to swear (an oath)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Testis iūrāvit sē vēra dīcere. The witness swore that he was telling the truth."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "iūrō, iūrāre, iūrāvī, iūrātum, with accusative and infinitive. English jury comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "iūrō is built on iūs, \"right, law\" (EtymDB): to bind oneself by law. English jury comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
